From 3e1d22bf61dcee996abefb131f522322f733c41a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edvinas Stunzenas <edvinas.stunzenas@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 11:48:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mac80211: do not seed a mesh peer's management replay
 counter from the RSC

An 802.11s link can stall for a minute or more after wpa_supplicant restarts
on one node: both ends report plink ESTAB and unicast keeps flowing, but path
selection never completes. One side's mpath to the peer sits at
MESH_PATH_RESOLVING with a zero next hop and its frame queue at the cap, while
the other side's mpath table stays empty. It clears on its own with no
userspace event, which makes it look like an MPM or key-exchange problem. It
is neither.

A mesh peer advertises the receive counter for its group key in the AMPE
GTKdata Key RSC. That value is sampled from the peer's group key transmit
counter, which does not track the packet numbers its group-addressed
management frames actually carry -- the two run as separate sequences and
drift apart with uptime. ieee80211_key_alloc() seeds all IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS+1
CCMP replay slots from that one value, including the slot management frames
use (rx.c sets security_idx = IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS for them). The management
slot can therefore start ahead of the frames it has to validate, and
ieee80211_crypto_ccmp_decrypt() then rejects every group-addressed HWMP frame
from the peer with RX_DROP_U_REPLAY until the peer's counter catches up.

Measured on a two-node mesh with the stall reproduced deliberately: the
installed counter was 0x1883 while the peer's PREQs carried 0x182C..0x1858,
and 44 consecutive frames were dropped 1:1 against the key's replay counter
over 38.8s, with not one reaching mesh_rx_path_sel_frame(). No PREQ means no
path and no PREP, which is exactly the observed state on both sides. Unicast
is unaffected because it uses the pairwise key, whose counter is untouched --
hence a link that looks alive while discovery is dead.

Leave the management slot at zero for a mesh peer's group key. The data slots
keep the advertised RSC. This gives up replay protection on group-addressed
robust management frames from that peer, which is the narrowest price that
removes the stall, and it does not depend on which frames the driver marks
RX_FLAG_PN_VALIDATED -- with the slot never seeded, the check cannot reject on
a stale value.

This is a workaround for peers advertising an RSC that does not describe the
sequence their management frames use. The correct fix is to advertise the
right value; once peers do, this should be reverted, as it would otherwise
keep discarding a then-correct RSC.

ref: 8devices/software/yocto/meta-8dev-qcs405#881

Signed-off-by: Edvinas Stunzenas <edvinas@8devices.com>
Upstream-Status: Backport

---
 net/mac80211/key.c | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 43 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/mac80211/key.c b/net/mac80211/key.c
index f5f1eb87797a..bbd7f617b940 100644
--- a/net/mac80211/key.c
+++ b/net/mac80211/key.c
@@ -842,6 +842,44 @@ static bool ieee80211_key_identical(struct ieee80211_sub_if_data *sdata,
 	return !crypto_memneq(tk_old, tk_new, new->conf.keylen);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A mesh peer advertises the receive counter for its group key in the AMPE
+ * GTKdata Key RSC. That value is sampled from the peer's group key transmit
+ * counter, which does not track the packet numbers its group-addressed
+ * *management* frames actually carry -- the two run as separate sequences and
+ * drift apart. Seeding the management replay slot from the RSC can therefore
+ * start that slot ahead of the frames it has to validate, and every
+ * group-addressed HWMP frame from the peer is then rejected as a replay
+ * (RX_DROP_U_REPLAY) until the peer's counter catches up. No PREQ reaches
+ * mesh_rx_path_sel_frame(), so no path is created and no PREP is sent, while
+ * both ends still report an established peering and unicast traffic -- which
+ * uses the pairwise key -- keeps flowing.
+ *
+ * Leave the management slot at zero for a mesh peer's group key; the data
+ * slots keep the advertised RSC. This gives up replay protection on
+ * group-addressed robust management frames from that peer, which is the
+ * narrowest price that removes the stall.
+ *
+ * This is a workaround for the peer advertising an RSC that does not describe
+ * the sequence its management frames use. The proper fix is to advertise the
+ * right value; once peers do that, this should be reverted.
+ */
+static void ieee80211_mesh_clear_mgmt_replay_ctr(struct ieee80211_key *key)
+{
+	switch (key->conf.cipher) {
+	case WLAN_CIPHER_SUITE_CCMP:
+	case WLAN_CIPHER_SUITE_CCMP_256:
+		memset(key->u.ccmp.rx_pn[IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS], 0,
+		       sizeof(key->u.ccmp.rx_pn[IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS]));
+		break;
+	case WLAN_CIPHER_SUITE_GCMP:
+	case WLAN_CIPHER_SUITE_GCMP_256:
+		memset(key->u.gcmp.rx_pn[IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS], 0,
+		       sizeof(key->u.gcmp.rx_pn[IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS]));
+		break;
+	}
+}
+
 int ieee80211_key_link(struct ieee80211_key *key,
 		       struct ieee80211_link_data *link,
 		       struct sta_info *sta)
@@ -861,6 +899,11 @@ int ieee80211_key_link(struct ieee80211_key *key,
 
 	mutex_lock(&sdata->local->key_mtx);
 
+	/* see ieee80211_mesh_clear_mgmt_replay_ctr() */
+	if (sta && !pairwise &&
+	    sdata->vif.type == NL80211_IFTYPE_MESH_POINT)
+		ieee80211_mesh_clear_mgmt_replay_ctr(key);
+
 	if (sta && pairwise) {
 		struct ieee80211_key *alt_key;
 
